Date: Fri, 1 Oct 93 00:39:38 -0700
From: dasher@netcom.com (D. Anton Sherwood)
Subject: rhetoric: ad hom vs truth

Two of you -- and it doesn't matter who, though we all know -- wrote:
> > An ad hominem attack is one that attacks your person. I have not
> > attacked your person.
 > Accusing me of looking like a fool is a personal attack . . . .

> > If your statements make you look foolish, that is your own fault, not
> > mine.
 > How my statements make me look is an abusive ad hominem attack which has
 > no bearing upon their truth.

It bears on their effectiveness.  The purpose of argument, as such, is not
merely to generate true statements, but to convince (or negotiate subsets
or rephrasings of each party's position on which the other can agree).
Which would you prefer: that the other judge some of your statements as
foolish, and silently allow that judgement of your style to taint his
judgement of your other statements; or that he identify the damage you
are doing to your argument, and by criticism invite you to correct it?

I've recently made the same type of "attack ad hominem" at James Donald,
saying something like "I wish you wouldn't say that, it makes you sound
crazy."  Now, almost nothing (for exceptions, see a.s.e) would make me
happier than to find a way to agree with JamesDon -- therefore I want
his argument to make sense.  My criticism here -- not argument -- is
intended as constructive.

