Date: Fri, 1 Oct 93 1:07:26 PDT
From: Eli Brandt <ebrandt@jarthur.Claremont.EDU>
Subject: HEALTH: Burning your Health Card

> From: Charlie Stross <charless@sco.com>
> As far as I can tell, medical prices in the US are driven up by two
> things: the overheads of an insurance system which needs to make a profit
> on its business, and the ridiculous costs of treatment.

The insurance bureaucracy does more than take its cut directly, it
adds enough paperwork to make each practitioner have a couple of
people shuffling cellulose full-time.  Furthermore, insurance
companies seem to have no particular interest in the cost of medical
procedures.  They set allowed rates, but they're not aggressive
about it; they just track Medicare allowed amounts.  One would think
that an insurance company could set lower fees, still be accepted by
a majority of doctors, and rake in policies.  Hmm, actually -- there
may be a law that requires doctors to accept certain private
insurers... I can't remember the situation, but in that case lowering
of fees would probably be verboten.  Nice zero-competition guaranteed
market there for insurers, and physicians can count on stable fees;
Joe Public is the only loser.  (I should stress that I don't remember
if this is in fact the situation -- I've had it explained to me
several times, and I still can't keep it straight).

Malpractice insurance is a significant piece of overhead, but it's
not what's causing the problem in general.  In fields such as ob/gyn,
where a fair number of people have been driven out by malpractice
costs, prices would have been driven up by reduced supply.  But since
people who pay for ob/gyn generally do so with insurance, fees are
fixed, and there is no effect.

> It seems (to my somewhat naive eye) that the health thing would be much
> less of a big deal if the costs were realistic. Whence the huge inflation
> in treatment costs? Well, again, historically it seems to be connected
> with the arrogation of the "right" to treat illness to a professional
> elite.

This, of course, is a significant factor.  Are there any studies
estimating the effect that this monopoly would have on health-care
prices in a free market?

> the alternative medical community in action, and while I wouldn't go to
> a homeopath with a broken leg or to an osteopath with cancer, they're
> far more effective at dealing with psychosomatic illnesses (including
> stress related problems, which account for a hell of a lot of days off work 
> these days) than conventional "big medicine" practitioners.

I agree that aromatherapy is at least as effective for a viral cold
as is penicillin (my roommate's HMO recently perpetrated the
latter), and safer as well.  However, I really wish there were some
adoption of, well, appropriate witch-doctor technology by people who
don't have to hang so much metaphysical baggage off it.  Homeopathy
and crystalloenergetic healing have decidedly counterfactual
premises; I submit that it is a Bad Thing to have a large fraction
of the population develop a lot of emotional involvement with some
bogosity about the unusual pharmacological properties of pure water
with a given history.

> More to the point, why don't we abolish hospitals?

Hospitals do have the problems that you mention.  However, much of
modern medical technology simply requires a lot of infrastructure.
And people, "deathist" though they may be, will predictably demand
the shiniest technology when their life is on the line -- or even
when they're irredeemably on their way to warm or cold storage.
They don't want to pay for it, of course.  But as long as they have
insurance, they don't have to, and will show up very unhappy on
national TV if they are denied it.

It would be nice if insurance companies had more finely granular
policies.  This one covers everything, that one doesn't include
life support after brain death, this one covers only herbal
pharmaceuticals, that one covers cryonics... They really can't,
of course, because there'd be "Evil Insurance Co." headlines the
first time somebody raised a ruckus after going over the fine
print and finding that they saved a dime by foregoing coverage of
doomed Siamese-twin separation.

> The whole practice of medicine in the Western world today is repugnant --
> hierarchical, elitist, exclusivist, and overpriced. Clinton is trying to
> fix it, whether or not you agree with his method. (I don't.) But when
> something is this broken, there comes a time when fixing it is a negative
> sum game. Maybe it's time to ditch the whole thing and start over ...

It's broken, but this is Clinton's fix is no fix.  Unfortunately, no
real fix could possibly make it politically.  It's the same old
problem...  We need some incremental steps that can be taken
independently of government screwing-around.  Unfortunately, the
proposed screwage-around looks to make this even more difficult than
it is now.

