Date: Fri, 01 Oct 93 10:44:48 EST
From: trestrab@GVSU.EDU (BETH TRESTRAIL)
Subject: Some US Monetary History   was: Syrian Counterfeit Money

There have been several posts on the list about FRNs, gold, & silver.
The value of gold was $20.67 throughout most of the 19th cent., with
minor fluctuations and adjustments in the pre-1850 era due to
problems with the US bimetallic standard. Roosevelt devalued the
dollar several times, settling at $35. Nixon devalued the dollar to
$42 before pulling the plug on convertability altogether.

The value of silver fluctuated greatly (rather, there was a two-tier
market for silver during the latter half of the 19th cent.). The free
market price was in the range of $.50/tr. oz. , while the (limited)
coinage rate was $1.29 (based on the AG $, as the minor coins had a
lower content). That is why there was major political strife over
"free coinage", with the west favoring it and the eastern bankers
in opposition. During the period 1878-96 the Federal government
was required to buy a varying amount of silver at the coinage rate,
with the amount varying with the relative political strength of the
two factions. The remainder of the silver produced had to be sold at
the significantly lower free market price. The cessation of the two
tier system in 1896 resulted in W J Bryant's "Cross of gold" speech.

US Notes appeared during the Civil War, as Lincoln did not wish to pay
the bankers interest on their gold in order to pay for the war. He
issued the "notes" in place of gold and silver "certificates" which
were the standard. The issuance of US Notes was limited to a fixed
amount due to compromise between Lincoln's faction and the bankers
and still circulate today, mostly in the form of $2 bills.

There were also issues of "National Currency" in the period 1914-1929,
which were issued by individual banks based on securities on deposit
at the Federal Reserve. Originally, this was limited to Treasury
securities but the standards were loosened over time to include
corporate securities and real estate loans.

In regard to legal tender, many contracts calling for repayment in
gold were unilaterally abrogated by Roosevelts action in 1933-34; many
lawsuits were filed against the railroads in particular demanding
payment of gold as called for in the bonds issued by them. These suits
were generally thrown out "as contrary to public monetary policy" as
determined by the Treasury dept. Only the government paid off its
bonds in gold, and then only to foreigners, it being illegal for US
citizens to own it by presidential fiat.

Hope this background info helps. I get the list in digest format, so
if anyone has any questions they want me to look at before this
evening, Please cc: me as well as sending to the list.


