Date: Fri, 1 Oct 1993 16:57:39 +0100 (BST)
From: Charlie Stross <charless@sco.com>
Subject: DOSSIER: The Net Has Ears, get used to it.

 Apropos the dossier business ...

I know some of you are probably skeptical, but I had a first-hand opportunity
to test it out last night. I'm working in Santa Cruz for two weeks -- my first
ever trip to the US, FYI -- so I mailed Tim and we went out for supper. In
return for a sum which I shall refrain from disclosing, he showed me 
my dossier.

I expected it to be large -- my outgoing mailbox stacks up about 80,000 words
of semantic content per month (that's about 450Kb for those of you who can't 
distinguish low-entropy text from uuencoded binaries) -- but what startled me
was how high a proportion of my mail he had. I didn't realize that so much
of my mail is cc'd to more than one person and eventually finds its way
out. In general I've made a point for the past few years of not talking in
mail about _anything_ I'd be embarrassed to be asked about by a police 
officer -- that is, anything more questionable than a minor misdemeanour --
but I'm shocked at the amount of information that a smart analyst could
infer about me from some of the stuff in the dossier. I'd forgotten all about
some of those postings to now-defunct mail lists. By developing a picture
from the overlaps between descriptive postings in places like alt.peeves 
(a pseudo-social newsgroup I tend to hang out in) a determined investigator
could work out a _lot_ about my lifestyle, interests, likes, dislikes,
degree of economic leverage, political opinions, and general subversive 
tendencies. And by analysing the postings to which I added follow-ups
they could probably net a rare bagful of similarly or oppositely
inclined people.

Friends, intelligence analysis is the commercial wave of the future -- and 
dossiers of net postings (compiled using I know not what kind of knowbots
or cheap labor) are grist for the mill of any such operation. I just know
that I don't want to see this kind of information in the hands of, say, 
GCHQ (the UK's equivalent of NSA). Oops, too late ...



